R174	Speaker Arg1:T123 Arg2:T121	
T124	Quotation 13109 13267	"It has long been an axiom of mine that the little things are infinitely the most important. Can you remember any other little things about Mr. Hosmer Angel?"
R175	Cueing Arg1:T122 Arg2:T124	
R176	Speaker Arg1:T123 Arg2:T124	
R177	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T121	
R178	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T124	
T125	Quotation 13268 13775	"He was a very shy man, Mr. Holmes. He would rather walk with me in the evening than in the daylight, for he said that he hated to be conspicuous. Very retiring and gentlemanly he was. Even his voice was gentle. He'd had the quinsy and swollen glands when he was young, he told me, and it had left him with a weak throat, and a hesitating, whispering fashion of speech. He was always well dressed, very neat and plain, but his eyes were weak, just as mine are, and he wore tinted glasses against the glare."
R179	Addressee Arg1:T123 Arg2:T125	
R180	Speaker Arg1:T90 Arg2:T125	
T126	Quotation 13776 13858	"Well, and what happened when Mr. Windibank, your stepfather, returned to France?"
R181	Speaker Arg1:T123 Arg2:T126	
R182	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T126	
T127	Quotation 13859 14791	"Mr. Hosmer Angel came to the house again and proposed that we should marry before father came back. He was in dreadful earnest and made me swear, with my hands on the Testament, that whatever happened I would always be true to him. Mother said he was quite right to make me swear, and that it was a sign of his passion. Mother was all in his favour from the first and was even fonder of him than I was. Then, when they talked of marrying within the week, I began to ask about father; but they both said never to mind about father, but just to tell him afterwards, and mother said she would make it all right with him. I didn't quite like that, Mr. Holmes. It seemed funny that I should ask his leave, as he was only a few years older than me; but I didn't want to do anything on the sly, so I wrote to father at Bordeaux, where the company has its French offices, but the letter came back to me on the very morning of the wedding."
R183	Addressee Arg1:T123 Arg2:T127	
R184	Speaker Arg1:T90 Arg2:T127	
T128	Quotation 14792 14814	"It missed him, then?"
R185	Speaker Arg1:T123 Arg2:T128	
R186	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T128	
T129	Quotation 14815 14880	"Yes, sir; for he had started to England just before it arrived."
R187	Addressee Arg1:T123 Arg2:T129	
R188	Speaker Arg1:T90 Arg2:T129	
T130	Quotation 14881 14981	"Ha! that was unfortunate. Your wedding was arranged, then, for the Friday. Was it to be in church?"
R189	Speaker Arg1:T123 Arg2:T130	
R190	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T130	
T131	Quotation 14982 15737	"Yes, sir, but very quietly. It was to be at St. Saviour's, near King's Cross, and we were to have breakfast afterwards at the St. Pancras Hotel. Hosmer came for us in a hansom, but as there were two of us he put us both into it and stepped himself into a four-wheeler, which happened to be the only other cab in the street. We got to the church first, and when the four-wheeler drove up we waited for him to step out, but he never did, and when the cabman got down from the box and looked there was no one there! The cabman said that he could not imagine what had become of him, for he had seen him get in with his own eyes. That was last Friday, Mr. Holmes, and I have never seen or heard anything since then to throw any light upon what became of him."
R191	Addressee Arg1:T123 Arg2:T131	
R192	Speaker Arg1:T90 Arg2:T131	
T132	Quotation 15738 15798	"It seems to me that you have been very shamefully treated,"
T133	Cue 15799 15803	said
T134	Entity 15804 15810	Holmes
T135	Quotation 15812 16221	"Oh, no, sir! He was too good and kind to leave me so. Why, all the morning he was saying to me that, whatever happened, I was to be true; and that even if something quite unforeseen occurred to separate us, I was always to remember that I was pledged to him, and that he would claim his pledge sooner or later. It seemed strange talk for a wedding-morning, but what has happened since gives a meaning to it."
R193	Cueing Arg1:T133 Arg2:T132	
R194	Speaker Arg1:T134 Arg2:T132	
R195	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T132	
R196	Addressee Arg1:T134 Arg2:T135	
R197	Speaker Arg1:T90 Arg2:T135	
T136	Quotation 16222 16328	"Most certainly it does. Your own opinion is, then, that some unforeseen catastrophe has occurred to him?"
R198	Speaker Arg1:T134 Arg2:T136	
R199	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T136	
T137	Quotation 16329 16464	"Yes, sir. I believe that he foresaw some danger, or else he would not have talked so. And then I think that what he foresaw happened."
R200	Addressee Arg1:T134 Arg2:T137	
R201	Speaker Arg1:T90 Arg2:T137	
T138	Quotation 16465 16520	"But you have no notion as to what it could have been?"
R202	Speaker Arg1:T134 Arg2:T138	
R203	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T138	
T139	Quotation 16521 16528	"None."
R204	Addressee Arg1:T134 Arg2:T139	
R205	Speaker Arg1:T90 Arg2:T139	
T140	Quotation 16529 16586	"One more question. How did your mother take the matter?"
R206	Speaker Arg1:T134 Arg2:T140	
R207	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T140	
T141	Quotation 16587 16659	"She was angry, and said that I was never to speak of the matter again."
R208	Speaker Arg1:T90 Arg2:T141	
R209	Addressee Arg1:T134 Arg2:T141	
T142	Quotation 16660 16696	"And your father? Did you tell him?"
R210	Speaker Arg1:T134 Arg2:T142	
R211	Addressee Arg1:T90 Arg2:T142	
T143	Entity 16808 16810	he
T144	Cue 16811 16815	said
T145	Quotation 16817 16912	what interest could anyone have in bringing me to the doors of the church, and then leaving me?
R212	Cueing Arg1:T144 Arg2:T145	
R213	Speaker Arg1:T143 Arg2:T145	
T146	Entity 16774 16775	I
R214	Addressee Arg1:T146 Arg2:T145	
T147	Quotation 16697 17260	"Yes; and he seemed to think, with me, that something had happened, and that I should hear of Hosmer again. As he said, what interest could anyone have in bringing me to the doors of the church, and then leaving me? Now, if he had borrowed my money, or if he had married me and got my money settled on him, there might be some reason, but Hosmer was very independent about money and never would look at a shilling of mine. And yet, what could have happened? And why could he not write? Oh, it drives me half-mad to think of it, and I can't sleep a wink at night."
T148	Entity 17261 17264	She
T149	Entity 17389 17395	Holmes
R215	Speaker Arg1:T148 Arg2:T147	
R216	Addressee Arg1:T149 Arg2:T147	
T150	Quotation 17344 17383	"I shall glance into the case for you,"
T151	Cue 17384 17388	said
T152	Quotation 17405 17658	"and I have no doubt that we shall reach some definite result. Let the weight of the matter rest upon me now, and do not let your mind dwell upon it further. Above all, try to let Mr. Hosmer Angel vanish from your memory, as he has done from your life."
R217	Cueing Arg1:T151 Arg2:T150	
R218	Cueing Arg1:T151 Arg2:T152	
R219	Speaker Arg1:T149 Arg2:T150	
R220	Speaker Arg1:T149 Arg2:T152	
R221	Addressee Arg1:T148 Arg2:T150	
R222	Addressee Arg1:T148 Arg2:T152	
T153	Quotation 17659 17701	"Then you don't think I'll see him again?"
R223	Addressee Arg1:T149 Arg2:T153	
R224	Speaker Arg1:T148 Arg2:T153	
T154	Quotation 17702 17715	"I fear not."
R225	Addressee Arg1:T148 Arg2:T154	
R226	Speaker Arg1:T149 Arg2:T154	
T155	Quotation 17716 17748	"Then what has happened to him?"
R227	Addressee Arg1:T149 Arg2:T155	
R228	Speaker Arg1:T148 Arg2:T155	
T156	Quotation 17749 17881	"You will leave that question in my hands. I should like an accurate description of him and any letters of his which you can spare."
R229	Speaker Arg1:T149 Arg2:T156	
T157	Quotation 17882 17934	"I advertised for him in last Saturday's Chronicle,"
T158	Cue 17935 17939	said
T159	Entity 17940 17943	she
R230	Cueing Arg1:T158 Arg2:T157	
R231	Speaker Arg1:T159 Arg2:T157	
R232	Addressee Arg1:T159 Arg2:T156	
R233	Addressee Arg1:T149 Arg2:T157	
T160	Quotation 17945 17999	"Here is the slip and here are four letters from him."
R234	Cueing Arg1:T158 Arg2:T160	
R235	Speaker Arg1:T159 Arg2:T160	
R236	Addressee Arg1:T149 Arg2:T160	
T161	Quotation 18000 18030	"Thank you. And your address?"
R237	Addressee Arg1:T159 Arg2:T161	
R238	Speaker Arg1:T149 Arg2:T161	
T162	Quotation 18031 18063	"No. 31 Lyon Place, Camberwell."
R239	Speaker Arg1:T159 Arg2:T162	
R240	Addressee Arg1:T149 Arg2:T162	
